    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "clinical features such as stiffness/spasms/ hyperlordosis, positive antibodies such as anti-GAD, EMG showing continuous motor-unit activity, response to benzodiazepines",
      "quote": "The diagnosis of SPS or SLS is clinical, based on the afore-mentioned symptomatology, the exclusion of other neurological diseases that could explain stiffness and rigidity, and the absence of parkinsonism and corticospinal tract involvement signs [4]. Electrophysiological recordings can show low-frequency motor unit firing at rest, in spite of voluntary relaxation, simultaneously from agonist and antagonist muscles. The diagnosis is aided by normal MRI imaging of the brain and spinal cord and the presence of high-titer antibodies against glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD)-65 is sometimes found (80% of SPS and 15% of SLS) [1,4].",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
